(Stage 1b safety, superseded by docs/BUGS_FOUND.md #115. Reading an
 element out of a list that widened to Mixed yields a value whose type
 the compiler cannot prove statically. Before #114/#115, the compiler
 refused to trust the receiving variable's declared type as a slot tag
 here at all, because nothing had ever verified the payload actually
 matched it: writing TAG_STRING over the integer 42 made the
 tag-dispatching printer treat 42 as a C-string pointer and dereference
 it, a segfault. The old safety net fell back to the integer tag instead
 - avoiding the crash, but at the cost of silently mislabelling `s`, a
 declared `text`, as a number wherever it was read in a mixed context.
 #115's general cast now runs at `s`'s own declaration, so the payload
 GENUINELY matches its declared type - `s` holds the text "42", not the
 integer 42 - and it is `s`'s real, cast, declared type that a later
 mixed-context read (the append below) sees, not a number.)

a list called m is ["a", "b"].
append 42 to m.
a text called s is element 3 of m.
a list called out is [].
append s to out.
print m.
print out.
